{
  "dom_id": "step-e3600",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Intervals on Which a Trigonometric Function Is Continuous",
  "problem": "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?",
  "worked_solution": "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi],$ but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at $x=0, \\pi, 2\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi),$ namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
